  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "In mid-13th-century England the silver penny was the only coin in everyday circulation; round halfpennies and farthings were introduced in Edward I's recoinage (from 1279–80). Rare earlier round halfpennies exist (Henry I, Henry III c.1220–22).",
      "sources": [
        "S1",
        "S2",
        "S3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "Before 1279 pennies were cut into halves and quarters ('cut halfpennies', 'cut farthings') and these circulated as small change; many were probably cut at the mint.",
      "sources": [
        "S2",
        "S4",
        "S3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "The Voided Long Cross penny (1247) extended the cross to the edge of the coin, mainly to deter clipping. Cut pieces follow the cross; the claim that the cross was designed as a cutting guide is NOT established and is not made.",
      "sources": [
        "S5",
        "S6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "'Farthing' derives from Old English feorthing, 'a fourth part'.",
      "sources": [
        "S7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "type": "estimate",
      "text": "An unskilled labourer in late-13th-century England earned roughly 1d–1½d a day (sometimes 2d); much farm work was paid partly in kind.",
      "sources": [
        "S8",
        "S9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "The Assize of Bread and Ale (c.1266–67, codifying older practice) fixed the weight of standard loaves such as the farthing loaf according to the price of wheat; short-weight bakers were amerced (fined) and repeat offenders could be pilloried.",
      "sources": [
        "S10",
        "S11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "Peasant houses in 13th-century England typically had an open central hearth without a chimney; smoke escaped through the roof.",
      "sources": [
        "S12",
        "S13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C7b",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "Timber framing (cruck framing in the Midlands, North and West), wattle-and-daub walls and thatched roofs were standard peasant building materials.",
      "sources": [
        "S12",
        "S13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C8",
      "type": "plausible",
      "text": "Some peasant houses (longhouses, e.g. Hound Tor, Dartmoor) sheltered animals under the same roof; a hen indoors in this house is a reasonable reconstruction, not documented for any particular house.",
      "sources": [
        "S14"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C9",
      "type": "estimate",
      "text": "Around 1300 roughly 80–85% of England's population lived in the countryside (towns c.15–20%: Britnell, Dyer).",
      "sources": [
        "S15",
        "S16"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C10",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "Unfree tenants (villeins) owed labour services ('week-work') on the lord's demesne as part of their rent, often two or three days a week and more at harvest, organised by the reeve; obligations varied widely by manor.",
      "sources": [
        "S17",
        "S18"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C11",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "Weekly markets were granted to lords by royal charter; many were granted under Henry III (e.g. Queen Camel 1264). Many villages had no market of their own.",
      "sources": [
        "S19"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C12",
      "type": "illustrative",
      "text": "The protagonist, Hugh the baker, the hen, the dialogue and this particular day are fictional; the coin-cutting scene dramatises a documented practice.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "id": "C13",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "Farthing loaves were a standard unit under the Assize of Bread.",
      "sources": [
        "S10",
        "S11"
      ]
    }
  ],
